## 2. Strategic Positioning

### 2.1 The Core Problem

Organizations generate massive volumes of behavioral signals daily. The failure mode is not lack of data — it is lack of correlation across disconnected sources, and lack of structured multidisciplinary capacity to act on early warnings before they escalate.

As of April 2024, 85% of U.S. schools reported having a threat assessment team, yet critical warning signals remain distributed across disconnected sources and often go unnoticed until an incident occurs. [Learning Policy Institute](https://learningpolicyinstitute.org/product/behavioral-threat-assessments-report) The same fragmentation afflicts corporations, transportation networks, hospitals, and public institutions globally.

The platform addresses two simultaneous failures: the technical failure of signal correlation at scale, and the organizational failure of multidisciplinary teams lacking structured tools to collaborate, document, and act consistently.

### 2.2 Operating Philosophy — Six Principles

**Human primacy.** AI detects. Humans decide. No consequence flows from AI output alone. Every alert is a recommendation to a trained reviewer. All actions affecting individuals require explicit human authorization, documented rationale, and supervisory accountability.

**Multidisciplinary integration.** The platform serves not just security personnel, but mental health professionals, educators, social workers, legal counsel, and administrators. Workflows are designed around team collaboration and supportive intervention, not just escalation.

**Privacy by design.** Data minimization, purpose limitation, pseudonymization, and retention controls are built into the pipeline at the point of ingestion — not added as compliance afterthoughts.

**Explainability first.** Every alert carries supporting evidence, confidence scores, SHAP-based feature attribution, and human-readable reasoning. No black-box outputs reach human reviewers.

**Bias as a permanent operational responsibility.** Fairness audits, disparate impact analysis, and bias safeguards are not launch tasks. They are continuous operational requirements with automatic enforcement thresholds.

**Auditability always.** Every system action, model inference, analyst decision, and case outcome is immutably logged with full provenance, creating a defensible chain of custody for legal, ethical, and institutional review.

### 2.3 Regulatory Alignment

The platform is classified as a **high-risk AI system** under EU AI Act Annex III and must satisfy conformity assessment requirements before deployment. Compliance is non-optional and non-deferrable.

Regulatory frameworks governing design:

- **EU AI Act (Annex III, High-Risk)** — mandatory human oversight mechanisms, transparency documentation, conformity assessment, and strict prohibitions on real-time biometric identification and emotion inference in institutional settings.
- **GDPR / CCPA** — data minimization, purpose limitation, right to erasure automation, consent frameworks, and data sovereignty options.
- **NIST SP 800-207** — Zero Trust Architecture principles applied internally across all service-to-service communication.
- **DHS BTAM Guidelines** — behavioral threat assessment and management is a systematic, evidence-based process emphasizing multidisciplinary teams, privacy, civil rights, and civil liberties consistent with legal authorities and policy. [Homeland Security](https://www.dhs.gov/behavioral-threat-assessment-and-management)
- **FERPA** (university deployments), **HIPAA** (health record adjacency), **CCPA**, and applicable state-level threat assessment mandates.
- **Secret Service NTAC Guidelines** and **CSTAG (Comprehensive School Threat Assessment Guidelines)** for institutional deployment configurations.

