## 3. Multidisciplinary Human Oversight Framework

This section is elevated to a top-level chapter in v2.0 because purely technical systems — however well-designed — risk surveillance creep, punitive misuse, and inconsistent application when not grounded in real multidisciplinary team dynamics.

### 3.1 The BTAM Team Model

The platform is designed to serve a **Behavioral Threat Assessment and Management (BTAM) team**, not individual analysts. That team must include, at minimum:

- Behavioral/mental health clinician
- Institutional administrator (dean, HR director, or equivalent)
- Legal counsel or compliance officer
- Law enforcement liaison (where institutionally appropriate)
- Social worker or student/employee support specialist
- Privacy officer (standing member, not ad hoc)

BTAM teams use best practices from many disciplines, including the public health-informed approach to violence prevention, emphasizing long-term prevention efforts guided by a systematic approach. [Homeland Security](https://www.dhs.gov/behavioral-threat-assessment-and-management)

The AI platform augments this team's capacity. It does not replace the team's judgment, professional expertise, or ethical responsibility.

### 3.2 Role-Specific Dashboard Views

The human dashboard (Layer 6) provides role-differentiated views:

- **Mental health clinician view:** Emphasis on behavioral timeline, support history, contextual factors, and recommended intervention pathways. Alerts surface welfare indicators alongside risk signals.
- **Administrator view:** Case status, intervention tracking, documentation for institutional policy compliance, and outcome logging.
- **Legal/compliance view:** Audit trail, data provenance, consent status, and case defensibility documentation.
- **Law enforcement liaison view:** Restricted to cases formally escalated with documented legal basis. No speculative access to unescalated alerts.
- **Security analyst view:** Full alert queue, risk scoring details, evidence packages, and case assignment.
- **Supervisor/auditor view:** Team workload, SLA compliance, bias metrics, and override audit logs.

### 3.3 Supportive Intervention Workflows

A critical design requirement absent from v1.0: the case management system must support **pathways to support services**, not only pathways to security escalation.

Case resolution options include:

- Referral to counseling or mental health services
- Referral to student/employee support programs
- Conflict mediation workflow
- Academic or workplace accommodation coordination
- Formal security escalation (requires senior reviewer authorization)
- Case closure with documented rationale

This directly implements the evidence base: behavioral threat assessment as an alternative to counterproductive zero-tolerance discipline policies can prevent violence in schools, and the U.S. Departments of Education, Justice, and Homeland Security all recommend BTA as a multi-disciplinary, collaborative approach. [Navigate360](https://navigate360.com/blog/behavioral-threat-assessment-checklist-training-more/)

The system must make referring to a counselor as easy and visible as escalating to security.

### 3.4 Multi-Tiered System of Support (MTSS) Integration

For institutional deployments, the platform includes integration hooks for MTSS frameworks — allowing behavioral risk signals to inform tiered support allocation (universal, targeted, intensive) rather than binary safe/unsafe categorizations. This positions the platform as a **preventive support tool** rather than a surveillance apparatus.

### 3.5 Governance Board — Mandatory Composition

The platform may not operate without an active governance board. Required members:

- Privacy officer (chair authority on data source approvals)
- Legal counsel
- Mental health / behavioral science expert
- External civil liberties advisor (independent, not institutional employee)
- Institutional ethics representative
- Operational security lead
- Community or faculty/staff representative (rotating)

The board meets quarterly at minimum, reviews aggregate system performance, bias audit results, case outcome data, and any proposed expansion of data sources or analytical capabilities. **No expansion is permitted without board approval.**

