### Layer 4 — AI Analysis Engine

**Purpose:** Detect meaningful patterns, anomalies, and threat signals from the normalized event stream and behavioral graph, using a multi-model parallel architecture with explainability built into every output.

#### 4.1 Behavioral Baseline & Anomaly Detection

UEBA solutions establish dynamic baselines by continuously monitoring patterns such as logins, access times, system usage, and data transfers. The system adapts these baselines over time as user roles, schedules, and normal activities evolve, ensuring detection remains relevant even in changing environments. [Deepwatch](https://www.deepwatch.com/glossary/user-and-entity-behavior-analytics-ueba/)

Deployment order is deliberate and risk-controlled — **statistical models before ML models:**

- **Stage 1 (launch):** Statistical Z-score and rule-based models. High interpretability, low false positive rate, immediately auditable. These become the baseline that all subsequent ML layers are benchmarked against.
- **Stage 2 (Phase 2):** Isolation Forest for access and attendance anomalies; DBSCAN for peer group behavioral norm clustering.
- **Stage 3 (Phase 3):** LSTM for temporal escalation trajectory modeling; supervised classifiers trained on labeled case outcomes.

This staged deployment directly addresses alert fatigue — the primary cause of analyst trust failure in UEBA implementations.

#### 4.2 NLP & Communication Signal Analysis

A combined ML/NLP pipeline processes events through parallel numerical and textual branches — anomaly detectors trained on structured characteristics and a hybrid NLP stack combining rules, TF-IDF, and character-level models — integrating results using a risk-aware policy that generates discrete risk levels with human-readable explanations. [MDPI](https://www.mdpi.com/2504-4990/8/1/25)

NLP modules target:
- Explicit threat language detection (rule-based + fine-tuned transformer classifier)
- Escalating hostility trajectory across message sequences over time
- Harassment and stalking pattern identification
- Sentiment drift detection per entity over configurable time windows
- Distress signal detection (for welfare-oriented alert generation, not only threat scoring)

**Model:** Fine-tuned BERT-class transformer, hosted entirely on-premise. **No communication content or PII-adjacent data is ever routed to external LLM APIs.** Where LLM-assisted fusion is used for temporal integration (see 4.3), it operates only on anonymized, non-PII event metadata.

#### 4.3 Advanced Multi-Modal Fusion

v2.0 advances the fusion layer beyond standard Transformer-based approaches to address complex inter-entity and temporal dynamics:

**Dynamic multi-view Graph Neural Networks (GNNs).** Separate graph views are maintained per data modality (physical access, communication signals, institutional records, operational data). A cross-view attention mechanism fuses these views dynamically, weighted by source reliability and temporal recency. This approach surfaces non-linear correlations across modalities that single-view models miss.

**Cross-modal attention.** When signals from independent modalities arrive within a configurable time window for the same entity, a cross-modal attention head amplifies the composite signal relative to single-source signals of equivalent severity. Two independent sources confirming the same pattern carry materially more weight than one source generating two data points.

**LLM-assisted temporal fusion (anonymized metadata only).** For cases involving complex multi-step behavioral sequences across weeks or months, a locally-hosted LLM assists in narrative coherence analysis of anonymized event metadata — helping analysts understand the arc of a situation, not just isolated data points. This capability operates exclusively on de-identified event type sequences and timestamps, never on content or identity.

#### 4.4 Graph-Based Relationship Analysis

Graph neural network analysis surfaces:
- Network proximity to previously flagged or case-resolved individuals
- Unusual relationship formation or dissolution patterns
- Communication network changes preceding known historical incidents (trained on anonymized, IRB-approved historical case data)
- Isolation patterns consistent with pathway-to-violence research
- Protective factor indicators (stable support relationships, active counseling engagement) that should **reduce** risk score contributions

The explicit modeling of protective factors is new in v2.0 — ensuring the system supports holistic assessment rather than purely threat-oriented scoring.

#### 4.5 Video Analytics

Computer vision pipeline runs on-premise GPU cluster. No video data is routed to cloud inference endpoints.

Capabilities:
- Crowd density and flow anomaly detection
- Perimeter intrusion detection
- Abandoned object detection
- Occupancy anomaly detection against established baseline patterns

**Explicit hard prohibitions (non-configurable):**
- Facial recognition of any kind
- Biometric identification from video
- Emotion inference or affect recognition — prohibited under EU AI Act Annex III for high-risk institutional deployments
- Behavioral classification of individuals from video without a specific triggered event context

#### 4.6 Model Governance

- All models versioned and registered in MLflow with full training lineage
- Scheduled re-evaluation against labeled ground truth (minimum quarterly)
- **Bias audits run quarterly** — results reported to governance board, with mandatory model review if disparity ratios exceed thresholds
- Model drift monitoring triggers automatic retraining proposals — human approval required before any retrained model is deployed to production
- Pre-deployment fairness testing required for every model release per EU AI Act high-risk requirements
- **Research integration hooks:** anonymized, aggregated, IRB-approved data sharing with academic partners to advance the field — with strict consent protocols and governance board approval per partnership

