### Layer 5 — Risk Intelligence Engine

**Purpose:** Synthesize multi-model outputs into coherent, prioritized, explainable, and bias-flagged risk assessments for human review.

#### 5.1 Risk Scoring Framework

Each entity receives a dynamic composite Risk Score (0–100), updated in real-time for high-severity triggers and on a configurable batch cadence for routine updates.

Score components:

```
Risk Score = f(
  Severity Weight       × Event Severity,
  Frequency Factor      × Recurrence Rate,
  Escalation Coeff.     × Temporal Trend Slope,
  Cross-Source Mult.    × Source Independence Score,
  Contextual Modifier   × Environmental Risk Factor,
  Protective Factor Adj.× Active Protective Signal Score  [NEW]
)
```

The Protective Factor Adjustment is new in v2.0. Confirmed engagement with support services, stable social relationships, and active case management reduce the composite score — preventing the system from functioning as a one-way ratchet that only increases risk scores.

**Alert tiers:**

| Score | Tier | Response |
|---|---|---|
| 0–30 | Monitor | Passive logging, no alert |
| 31–55 | Elevated | Analyst queue, 5-day review SLA |
| 56–74 | Concerning | Analyst notification, 48-hour review SLA, BTAM team awareness |
| 75–89 | High | Immediate analyst assignment, case opened, BTAM team convened |
| 90–100 | Critical | Immediate escalation to senior reviewer, duty officer, and BTAM team lead |

#### 5.2 Explainability (XAI) — Full Alert Package

The XAI-Compliance-by-Design framework routes explainability outputs and technical traces into structured, audit-ready evidence throughout the AI lifecycle, aligned with obligations under the EU AI Act and GDPR. [MDPI](https://www.mdpi.com/2624-800X/6/1/7)

Every alert package delivered to a human reviewer contains:

- Plain-language narrative summary of contributing signals (written for a multidisciplinary audience, not only security analysts)
- SHAP feature importance values for each contributing model component
- **Bias flag:** if any SHAP contribution is traceable to a feature that correlates with a protected characteristic (age, disability status, demographic group), this is surfaced explicitly with a prominence warning and automatic bias review flag
- Timeline of contributing events with full source attribution and consent status
- Confidence interval for the risk score, with uncertainty clearly expressed
- Data provenance chain: source system → connector → normalization → model → score
- Protective factors present (if any) and their score contribution
- Recommended investigative next steps (non-prescriptive, differentiated by BTAM role)
- What-if analysis: which signals, if resolved or explained, would reduce the score below alert threshold
- Intervention pathway options: support referral, mediation, accommodation, or escalation

#### 5.3 False Positive Management

False positive rate is the primary driver of analyst trust — and therefore the primary driver of real-world safety outcomes. It is treated as a first-class engineering and operational concern.

- Statistical/rule-based models are deployed first. ML-based detection is added incrementally only after statistical baselines are validated.
- Contextual override rules suppress alerts for known benign patterns (e.g., access anomalies explained by documented travel, building maintenance, authorized unusual hours).
- Analyst feedback on every alert outcome (true positive, false positive, inconclusive) feeds a labeled training dataset for supervised model refinement.
- **Target:** < 10% false positive rate for High-tier and above alerts, measured monthly.
- Alert deduplication prevents multiple alerts for the same underlying signal within a configurable suppression window.
- Suppression rules are version-controlled, auditable, and require privacy officer approval to modify.

